Let me look at the `format_skip` function again:

```c
const char *
format_skip(const char *s, const char *end)
{
        int     brackets = 0;

        for (; *s != '\0'; s++) {
                if (*s == '#' && s[1] == '{')
                        brackets++;
                if (*s == '#' &&
                    s[1] != '\0' &&
                    strchr(",#{}:", s[1]) != NULL) {
                        s++;
                        continue;
                }
                if (*s == '}')
                        brackets--;
                if (strchr(end, *s) != NULL && brackets == 0)
                        break;
        }
        if (*s == '\0')
                return (NULL);
        return (s);
}
```

The issue is in the `s[1]` read. When `s` points to the last character of the string (which is `#`), `s[1]` reads the null terminator. That's fine.

But wait - what if `s` points to the null terminator itself? The loop condition `*s != '\0'` prevents that.

